1
00:00:00,817 --> 00:00:09,130
Welcome to South America episode two of three. First, I clear the chosen names
for a fresh regional lesson.

2
00:00:09,788 --> 00:00:21,386
Four countries run down the Andean west of the continent: Ecuador on the
equator, Peru below it, Bolivia inland, and Chile the long thin strip along
the Pacific.

3
00:00:22,055 --> 00:00:25,074
Let's bring Ecuador and its neighbours into view.

4
00:00:28,010 --> 00:00:40,503
Ecuador straddles the equator on the Pacific coast, with Colombia to its north
and Peru to its south. The Galápagos Islands lie far out in the ocean to its
west.

5
00:00:41,158 --> 00:01:02,146
Its name comes from the equator, which crosses it just north of Quito. Darwin
visited the Galápagos in 1835 and the islands helped him to his theory of
evolution. Because the earth bulges at the equator, the summit of Chimborazo
is the point on its surface farthest from the earth's centre.

6
00:01:02,810 --> 00:01:12,829
Remember Ecuador as the country named for the line that crosses it, with the
Galápagos far offshore and Chimborazo the highest point from the centre of the
earth.

7
00:01:13,501 --> 00:01:16,136
Let's bring Peru and its neighbours into view.

8
00:01:18,563 --> 00:01:34,403
Peru runs down the Pacific coast below Ecuador, with Colombia to its north,
Brazil to its east and Bolivia and Chile inland to its south. Its western
shore holds a coastal desert, and the Andes rise behind it.

9
00:01:35,060 --> 00:01:57,920
Its name perhaps comes from Birú, the name of a chief or a river the Spanish
came across on the coast further north. Machu Picchu, the Inca city of the
15th century, was hardly known outside the region until 1911. The potato was
first grown in these Andes, and thousands of varieties are still grown here.

10
00:01:58,572 --> 00:02:07,779
Remember Peru as the Andean country of the potato: coastal desert in front,
high mountains behind, and Machu Picchu above the cloud line.

11
00:02:08,464 --> 00:02:11,529
Let's bring Bolivia and its neighbours into view.

12
00:02:13,787 --> 00:02:25,385
Bolivia is landlocked, in the middle of the continent, with Peru to its north,
Brazil to its east, Paraguay and Argentina to its south and Chile to its west.

13
00:02:26,041 --> 00:02:43,033
It is named for Simón Bolívar, who led its independence from Spain. La Paz,
the seat of government, is the highest capital in the world; Sucre is the
constitutional one. The Salar de Uyuni is the largest salt flat in the world.

14
00:02:43,689 --> 00:02:55,032
Remember Bolivia as the landlocked middle of the continent, named for its
liberator, with the world's largest salt flat and the world's highest capital.

15
00:02:55,727 --> 00:02:59,558
Let's bring Chile and its neighbours into view.

16
00:03:01,753 --> 00:03:18,855
Chile is a long thin country on the Pacific, about 4,300 kilometres long and
on average less than 200 wide, with the Andes along its eastern edge and
Argentina to its east.

17
00:03:19,505 --> 00:03:34,819
Where its name comes from is uncertain; one reading, from a native language,
is "where the land ends". The Atacama, in its north, is the driest desert in
the world outside the poles.

18
00:03:35,479 --> 00:03:44,175
Remember Chile as the ribbon of a country down the Pacific, with the driest
desert on Earth in its north and the Andes along its back.

19
00:03:47,965 --> 00:03:58,925
Ecuador is on the equator, Peru below it with Chile along the coast and
Bolivia inland. Chile runs the whole western edge down to the far south.

20
00:03:59,579 --> 00:04:10,458
Remember the Galápagos and Chimborazo, the potato and Machu Picchu, the
world's largest salt flat and highest capital, and the driest desert outside
the poles.
